course: dictionary
lesson: 4521
title: Headlight in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A headlight is a strong light at the front of a car, bus, bike or train. It helps the driver see the road at night and lets other people see the vehicle. In «At night a driver sees a child in dark clothes very late», the driver needs the headlights to see the child at all.
explain: Read the words around the word. In «reflective strips shine the headlights straight back to the driver», reflective is the clue. Reflective means it bounces light back, so the headlights shine on the child and the strips send the light back to the driver, who can see the child from far away.
explain: Headlight can also mean a light worn on the head, though this is more often called a headlamp or head torch. Use the context to choose. On a road, with a driver or a car, it means the vehicle light. On someone's head, it means the light they wear.
fact: A headlight is a strong light at the front of a vehicle.
fact: Headlights help the driver see the road at night, and in fog and rain.
fact: A headlight can also be a light worn on the head, also called a headlamp or head torch.
fact: Reflective clothes shine back in headlights, so a walker can be seen from far away at night.
fact: «Like a deer in headlights» means frozen with fear.
exercise: «Deer often freeze in bright headlights.» What does the sudden light do to a deer's eyes? | They warm the deer up | They make the deer hungry | *They dazzle the eyes, which are made for the dark | They make the deer fall asleep
exercise: In road safety, what does «like a deer in headlights» mean? | Happy and dancing | *Frozen with fear | Lost in the dark | Running very fast
exercise: A cyclist needs a light at the front. What colour should it be? | Blue | Red | *White | Green
test: Which light helps a driver see the road at night? | Street bins | Puddles | Road signs | *Headlights
test: Nadia says headlight must always mean a car light. Is she right? | No, it means a bright sunny day | Yes, it only ever means a car light | *No, it can also mean a light worn on the head | Yes, because it is a noun
exam: Why should a walker wear reflective clothes at night? | To stay warm in the rain | To look good in photos | So they can hear the cars better | *So headlights shine back and drivers can see them
